= Triphala (त्रिफला)

_A classical Ayurvedic compound formula — three Indian fruits combined in equal parts — and one of the most-used preparations in the saṃhitā tradition. Unlike Chyavanprash (which contains 40+ ingredients), Triphala is the_ minimal _classical compound: just three drugs, each_ Terminalia_-and-_Phyllanthus_-related, prepared by simple combination. Triphala is the canonical Ayurvedic preparation for daily detoxification, mild laxation, eye-tonification, and rasāyana use. Status: fully classical (in countless saṃhitā references), with the individual component drugs canonical and treated under their own names — see component-section below. Each of the three components is also a classical Bencao drug, though triphala_ as a compound _is Ayurvedic, not Chinese. Greco-Roman: not classical; the component drugs are South Asian._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Sanskrit:* त्रिफला (_tri-phalā_) — *"the three fruits"*; from त्रि (_tri_, "three") + फल (_phala_, "fruit"). The compound name encodes the formula's structure directly. Sometimes also त्रिफलादि (_triphalādi_, "triphala-plus") as a member of compound names.

*The three fruits, in canonical equal proportion (1:1:1 by weight, sometimes with adjusted ratios in specific applications):*

1. *आमलकी (_āmalakī_)* — *_Phyllanthus emblica_ L.* (older synonym _Emblica officinalis_), family Phyllanthaceae — *the Indian gooseberry*; the most-praised single fruit in Ayurvedic medicine; classical _rasāyana-vara_ ("best of rejuvenatives"); also called *āmlā*, *āṁvalā*; the basis of Chyavanprash. *Rasa:* five rasas without lavaṇa (sour-sweet-bitter-pungent-astringent); *Vīrya:* śīta (cooling); *Doṣa:* tridoṣa-pacifying with particular Pitta-pacification; *Karma:* rasāyana, cakṣuṣya, vitamin-C rich (~600 mg / fresh fruit), antioxidant, hepatic-supportive.

2. *विभीतकी (_vibhītakī_)* — *_Terminalia bellirica_ (Gaertn.) Roxb.*, family Combretaceae — *Beleric myrobalan*; also called *bahedā*. *Rasa:* astringent (kaṣāya, dominant); *Vīrya:* uṣṇa (warming); *Doṣa:* Kapha-pacifying primarily, with mild Vāta-tendency; *Karma:* bhedana (purgative, modest), respiratory-supportive (chronic cough, voice), eye-supportive.

3. *हरीतकी (_harītakī_)* — *_Terminalia chebula_ Retz.*, family Combretaceae — *Chebulic myrobalan*; the most-praised single drug in the Tibetan / Buddhist medical tradition (the Medicine Buddha holds harītakī fruit in iconography). *Rasa:* five rasas without lavaṇa (sweet-sour-bitter-pungent-astringent); *Vīrya:* uṣṇa (warming); *Doṣa:* tridoṣa-pacifying with Vāta-pacification especially; *Karma:* bhedana (purgative), āma-pācana, intelligent gentle laxative (the famous "mother" attribution — "harītakī is to mankind as a mother is to children"), rasāyana, eye-supportive, voice-supportive.

The *combined formula* gives a balanced doṣa profile through the convergent action of three different fruits with overlapping but complementary properties.#footnote[Component-drug identification: standard Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India, Part I, monographs on āmalakī, vibhītakī, harītakī; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_ (the very first chapter of the foundational Bhāvaprakāśa drug-classification, headed by harītakī — the prestige position). The Medicine Buddha holds harītakī: Tibetan Buddhist iconography. Translations: Murthy, _Bhāvaprakāśa_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Hindi:* त्रिफला (triphala).
*Chinese:* 三果 (sānguǒ) — modern; 三果湯 (sānguǒtāng) — modern; the Tibetan-medical equivalent is 三果丸 (sānguǒwán). Harītakī alone is 訶子 (hēzǐ), a *fully classical Chinese drug*; vibhītakī and āmalakī enter Chinese pharmacy chiefly through Tibetan-medical and Tang-period Buddhist contact.

== Classical origin and authority

Triphala is *not* introduced in Caraka as a single named compound the way Cyavanaprāśa is — the three drugs are individually praised throughout the saṃhitās, and the formula emerges in the *Sharangadhara Saṃhitā* and the broader medieval Ayurvedic compounding tradition as a canonical pairing. The *Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu* (16th c.) treats triphala as a standing compound. The formula has become *one of the most-prescribed Ayurvedic preparations in the modern era* — universal in modern Indian Ayurvedic practice and globally exported.#footnote[Triphala as a compound: Sharangadhara Saṃhitā, Madhyamakhaṇḍa (compound formulas); Bhāvaprakāśa, _madhyama khaṇḍa_; Astanga Hridaya and the broader medieval tradition. The component drugs are saṃhitā-classical (Caraka, Suśruta); the compound is medieval. _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I — standardized triphala recipe. Cross-system note: the Tibetan medical tradition uses the same three-fruit combination (_'bras bu gsum_, "three fruits") as a foundational formula.]

== Composition

The standardized recipe (per _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_):
- *Āmalakī* (dried āmalakī fruit, deseeded) — *1 part by weight*
- *Vibhītakī* (dried bellirica fruit, deseeded) — *1 part by weight*
- *Harītakī* (dried chebula fruit, deseeded) — *1 part by weight*

The three are powdered together to a fine churna and mixed; the resulting *Triphala churna* is the standard form. Variant ratios exist for specific applications (the eye-tonic _triphala-svarasa_ for ophthalmic use sometimes uses different proportions, with āmalakī predominating).#footnote[Standardized recipe: _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_, Part I, _Triphala cūrṇa_ monograph. Variant ratios: Sharangadhara Saṃhitā and modern Ayurvedic compounding literature.]

== Indications and Uses

The classical and modern Ayurvedic indications for triphala are exceptionally broad — the formula is a *foundational rasāyana* and a near-universal supportive preparation.

*Principal classical actions (saṃyukta):*
- *Tridoṣa-śāmaka* — balances all three doṣas through the convergent action of the three components.
- *Mṛdu-bhedana / mṛdu-virecana* — *gentle laxation / mild purgation* — *the canonical daily-bowel-regulator of Ayurveda*; classical use 3–5 g at bedtime with warm water for habitual mild constipation; effect is *gentle and non-habit-forming* (unlike the strong stimulant laxatives), distinguishing triphala from harsh purgatives.
- *Pācana, āma-pācana* — supports digestion of āma (undigested food / metabolic toxins); a foundational detoxification action.
- *Cakṣuṣya, netra-rasāyana* — *eye-tonic*; the canonical *triphala-svarasa* / triphala-decoction eye wash is one of the most-cited classical eye preparations; modern eye-drop products incorporate triphala extracts.
- *Rasāyana* — *rejuvenative*; long-term daily use is classically endorsed as a foundational longevity practice.
- *Krimi-ghna* — anthelminthic / antimicrobial.
- *Medo-haranā* — fat-reducing (modern functional-food register; modest support).
- *Pramehaghna* — supportive in _prameha_ (urinary disorders, diabetic-spectrum).
- *Varṇya, tvacya* — complexion / skin-supportive.
- *Hṛdya* — cardiotonic.
- *Vāk-svara-prada* — voice-improving (the harītakī contribution).

The classical phrase "_yathāsthitam api yathārtham_" — "according to the situation, also according to the need" — captures triphala's adaptive register: it acts as gentle laxative in constipation, as binding agent in mild diarrhea, as digestive in dyspepsia, as eye-tonic in eye irritation. The *adaptive/normalizing* character is the classical attribution.

*Doṣa effect:* tridoṣa-pacifying with subtle directional adjustment depending on individual doṣa state; āmalakī predominates the Pitta-pacification, harītakī the Vāta-pacification, vibhītakī the Kapha-pacification — together gentle on all three.

== Cautions and Viruddha

_(tagged by source tier)_

- *(S/N) Conditional cautions:*
  - *Severe dehydration / Vāta-extreme vacuity*: the bhedana action can aggravate.
  - *Pregnancy*: the bhedana / purgative action makes triphala *contraindicated in pregnancy* — classical and modern caution.
  - *Severe diarrhea*: not appropriate as a primary intervention (despite the adaptive use in mild diarrhea, severe dysentery requires specific therapy).
  - *Heavy depletion / severe debility*: cautious use; the gentle bhedana is generally fine but the cooling drying tendency requires consideration.

- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Drug interactions*: triphala's polyphenol content has theoretical interactions with iron supplements (binding), with some antibiotics, with anticoagulants (modest theoretical bleeding effect), and with hypoglycemic drugs.
  - *Sourcing*: commercial triphala varies in quality; reputable suppliers test for heavy metals (especially lead, which has been documented at low levels in some Indian commercial Ayurvedic powders) and pesticides.
  - *Bowel-pattern adaptation*: long-term daily use establishes a stable bowel pattern; sudden cessation may produce transient adjustment but not classical rebound constipation.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Triphala is among the_ cleanest cases of direct Ayurvedic-to-Tibb adoption _in materia medica history. The Sanskrit_ tri-phalā _("three fruits") is preserved in Arabic-Persian-Urdu phonology as_ Itrīfal _(اطریفل), and the formula is a canonical Unani preparation in multiple variants. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu:* اطریفل (_Itrīfal_) — direct phonetic adoption of Sanskrit _tri-phalā_; one of the rare cases where a Sanskrit compound name is preserved essentially unchanged in Arabic script. The three component fruits are individually treated in Tibb under their own names:
- *Harītakī = Halīlaj (هليلج)* — Avicennan canonical drug, the foundational chebulic myrobalan of medieval Arabic pharmacy; multiple varieties distinguished (_halīlaj aswad_, black; _halīlaj aṣfar_, yellow; _halīlaj kābulī_, Kabul; etc.).
- *Vibhītakī = Balīlaj (بليلج)* — Avicennan canonical drug.
- *Āmalakī = Amlaj (املج) / Anlaj* — Avicennan canonical drug.

*Mizāj of the compound:* *Cold and Dry in the 1st degree* (Mughal Unani consensus); the individual components mostly Hot-Dry but combination is balanced.

*Af'āl:* *Mulayyin* (gentle laxative — the canonical Itrīfal action, paralleling the Ayurvedic mṛdu-bhedana); *jālī* (cleansing); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *muqawwī al-baṣar* (eye-tonic — direct preservation of Ayurvedic cakṣuṣya); *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent); *mughayyir li-l-akhlāṭ* (humoral modifier); *muḥsin li-l-bashara*.

*Istemālāt:* habitual mild constipation; eye disorders; chronic skin disease; gastric weakness; humoral cleansing; the Tibb _rasāyana_-equivalent register.

*Mawāne':* pregnancy; severe diarrhea; severe dehydration; cold-pattern weakness.

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; rose-water; warming spices.

*Badal:* senna for stronger laxative; rhubarb (*rīwand*).

*Tibb formulas — the Itrīfal class:*
- *Itrīfal Ṣaghīr (إطريفل صغير, "Small Itrīfal")* — *direct equivalent of Ayurvedic Triphalā cūrṇa*; the basic three-fruit powder.
- *Itrīfal Kabīr* — large itrīfal, with added drugs.
- *Itrīfal Aftīmūn* (with _Cuscuta_ aftīmūn) — for melancholic / depressive conditions.
- *Itrīfal Mulayyan* — gentle-laxative form with added muṣliḥ.
- *Itrīfal Zamānī*, *Itrīfal Ushshāq*, *Itrīfal Muqil*, *Itrīfal Ghuddadī* — variants for specific indications (joint disease, lymph-node swelling, etc.).

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II — individual entries for halīlaj, balīlaj, amlaj; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _harr / harītakī / halīlaj_ and parallel entries; *Bayāz-e Kabīr*; CCRUM Unani Pharmacopoeia — Itrīfal class formulas are core Unani polyherbals.#footnote[Tibb Itrīfal: the Sanskrit-Arabic phonological adoption is one of the cleanest direct Indo-Arabic medical transfers, with the compound preserved essentially unchanged for ~1500 years across the two traditions.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *訶子 (Hēzǐ, harītakī alone)* is fully classical and appears in several Chinese formulas — see _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al.
- *三果丸 (Sānguǒwán)* in the Tibetan-medical / Tang-Buddhist tradition — the three-fruit pill.
- Triphala as compound *enters Chinese pharmacy chiefly through Tibetan medicine and Tang-period Buddhist transmission*, not through the indigenous Bencao tradition.

=== Ayurveda

- *Triphalā cūrṇa* — the simple powder, ~3–6 g at bedtime with warm water (the canonical preparation).
- *Triphalā kvātha* — decoction; ~50–100 mL.
- *Triphalā ghṛta* — medicated ghee; the classical _eye-tonic_ medicated ghee, used in *netra-tarpana* (eye-bath); for chronic eye disorders, including diabetic and degenerative retinal conditions.
- *Triphalā guggulu* — triphala + guggulu (Commiphora wightii); for chronic inflammation, weight management, lipid disorders.
- *Triphalā mandūra* — triphala + iron-calx; for anemia.
- *Triphalā-svarasa eye-wash* — decoction or filtered juice for daily eye irrigation; classical netra-vidhi.
- *External use as gargle and skin wash*.

=== Western / Modern

No Greco-Roman tradition. *Modern Western adoption* through Ayurveda-influenced wellness and integrative medicine; recent clinical research on antioxidant and adaptogen claims; substantial commercial / nutraceutical market.

== Modern Pharmacology

The combined formula's pharmacology is the sum of the three components.

*Key constituents (combined):*
- *Polyphenols and tannins* — exceptionally high; major contributors include *gallic acid*, *ellagic acid*, *chebulinic acid*, *chebulagic acid*, *corilagin*, *emblicanin A and B* (from āmalakī).
- *Vitamin C* — substantial from āmalakī (modern Indian whole-fruit āmalakī is among the richest natural vitamin C sources; cooked / dried āmalakī retains less but the polyphenols protect the residual better than for many vitamin-C foods).
- *Polysaccharides*.
- *Triterpenoids*.
- *Iron, calcium, manganese* — meaningful in dried-fruit fraction.

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antioxidant*: substantial in vitro; consistent across the polyphenol fraction; modest in vivo translation.
- *Anti-inflammatory*: preclinical broad.
- *Hepatoprotective*: preclinical evidence; some clinical evidence for liver function support.
- *Mild laxative*: preclinical and clinical evidence supports the gentle-bhedana action; anthraquinone-free, so not stimulant-laxative habit-forming.
- *Antimicrobial*: in vitro broad-spectrum.
- *Glycemic*: small clinical evidence for modest hypoglycemic effect.
- *Lipid-lowering*: small clinical evidence in dyslipidemia.
- *Dental / oral health*: triphala mouthwash has modest clinical evidence for plaque and gingivitis reduction; emerging clinical use.
- *Eye-care*: traditional eye-tonic use has modest preclinical and clinical support; the antioxidant fraction is the modern mechanism.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the classical _rasāyana_ + _cakṣuṣya_ + _mṛdu-bhedana_ + _pācana_ register aligns with the modern antioxidant + antimicrobial + mild laxative + cardiovascular-supportive pharmacology. The cross-system convergence is reasonable but the modern evidence base is dominated by small Indian trials of variable quality.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Classical adult dose*: 3–6 g powder at bedtime with warm water (the canonical preparation).
- *Pediatric dose (>5 years)*: ~1–3 g with appropriate vehicle.
- *Elderly*: 3–6 g, with monitoring for digestive tolerance.
- *Tablets / standardized extracts*: commonly 500 mg–2 g/day.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously or avoid)*: (a) *pregnancy* (avoid); (b) *severe dehydration*; (c) *active severe diarrhea*; (d) iron-supplement absorption (separate timing); (e) anticoagulant therapy (theoretical); (f) sourcing — verify heavy-metal-tested supplier.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

